<section id="section-tips" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <ArticleImage src={bangkokSkyline} alt="The Bangkok skyline with the BTS Skytrain and JC Tower in the foreground" caption="The Bangkok skyline" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Important Things to Know Before Visiting Bangkok</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Before diving into all the exciting things to do in Bangkok, make sure you read through these important tips. Knowing what to expect before your trip will help you avoid common travel surprises and make your time in Bangkok smoother and more enjoyable.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Bring Cash and Watch Out for ATM Fees</h3>
    <p>Although cards are accepted in many places, cash is still useful in Bangkok, especially at street food stalls, local markets and smaller shops. I&rsquo;d recommend bringing some cash with you or exchanging a small amount before your trip, so you have money available when you arrive.</p>
    <p>If you plan to withdraw Thai baht from ATMs, keep in mind that Thai banks charge a fee for foreign-card withdrawals, and your own bank may charge extra fees on top. Since the local fee applies to each withdrawal, it&rsquo;s worth checking the amount displayed on the screen before confirming and avoiding lots of small withdrawals.</p>
    <p>And here&rsquo;s one little thing to remember: don&rsquo;t forget your card! This might sound obvious, but it has happened to me, too. Some ATMs return your cash before returning your card, and it&rsquo;s surprisingly easy to grab your money and walk away while waiting for the card to come out.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Stay Hydrated in the Heat</h3>
    <p>Bangkok can get extremely hot and humid, especially during the hotter months, and walking around sightseeing can be more exhausting than you might expect. Make sure you drink water regularly, take breaks throughout the day and step into an air-conditioned caf&eacute; or shopping mall when you need to cool down.</p>
    <p>Electrolytes can also be useful if you&rsquo;re sweating heavily, particularly after spending long periods outdoors. Don&rsquo;t underestimate the heat, and try not to squeeze too many outdoor activities into the hottest part of the day.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Wear Comfortable Shoes That Are Easy to Remove</h3>
    <p>You&rsquo;ll probably do a lot of walking in Bangkok, so comfortable shoes are a must. I&rsquo;d also recommend wearing shoes or sandals that are easy to take off and put back on, as you&rsquo;ll need to remove them before entering many temple buildings.</p>
    <p>This might seem like a small detail, but when you&rsquo;re visiting several temples in one day, not having to struggle with your shoes every time makes things much easier.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Be Careful with Tap Water</h3>
    <p>It&rsquo;s best to stick to bottled or otherwise safely treated drinking water in Bangkok rather than drinking straight from the tap. If you prefer to be extra cautious, you can also use bottled water when brushing your teeth, especially if you have a sensitive stomach. Choose sealed bottles and avoid drinking water if you&rsquo;re unsure whether it&rsquo;s safe.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Bring a Face Mask for Air Pollution</h3>
    <p>You&rsquo;ll probably notice that many locals in Bangkok wear face masks, and during my first visit, I wondered why. I soon experienced just how bad the air pollution could get. The pollution levels were so high that I avoided going outside around midday and planned my days around early morning and late afternoon outings instead.</p>
    <p>I also remember that public transportation was made free for a week to encourage people to use the trains and buses instead of their own vehicles, as part of an effort to reduce PM2.5 pollution. This was a temporary measure introduced in January 2025, rather than a permanent policy.</p>
    <p>If you&rsquo;re visiting Bangkok, I&rsquo;d recommend checking the air quality during your stay and bringing a well-fitting N95 or KN95 mask for days when pollution levels are high. These masks help reduce exposure to fine particles. When air quality is poor, try to limit time outdoors and plan indoor activities instead.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Get an eSIM Before You Arrive</h3>
    <p>One thing I&rsquo;d recommend doing before your trip is setting up an eSIM. Personally, I use <a href="https://airalo.pxf.io/c/7435210/1268485/15608" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer sponsored" class={mapLink}>Airalo</a>, and it&rsquo;s been really convenient when traveling. Having mobile data as soon as you arrive makes it much easier to look up directions, get around the city, book a Grab, or find your way to your hotel without relying on airport Wi-Fi.</p>
    <p>I&rsquo;d recommend setting up your eSIM before your flight, so you&rsquo;re ready to go as soon as you land. Just make sure your phone supports eSIMs and check when your data plan becomes active.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Check Your Passport and Complete the Thailand Digital Arrival Card</h3>
    <p>Before flying to Thailand, make sure your passport meets the entry requirements for your nationality. Some travelers need at least six months of passport validity remaining, so check the current rules before your trip rather than leaving it until the last minute.</p>
    <p>You&rsquo;ll also need to complete the Thailand Digital Arrival Card (TDAC) before entering the country. It isn&rsquo;t a visa; it&rsquo;s an online arrival form required for foreign travelers entering Thailand. You can submit it within three days before your arrival, including your arrival date, through the official, free website: tdac.immigration.go.th.</p>
    <p>Make sure you use the official website, and remember that completing the TDAC doesn&rsquo;t replace any visa requirements that may apply to you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-airport" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <ArticleImage src={airportStatue} alt="A giant guardian statue at Suvarnabhumi Airport in Bangkok" caption="A giant guardian statue at Suvarnabhumi Airport" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">How to Get from the Airport to Bangkok</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If you&rsquo;re flying into Suvarnabhumi Airport (BKK), getting into central Bangkok is relatively easy, and you don&rsquo;t necessarily need to take a taxi. The Airport Rail Link connects the airport to the city and is a convenient option, especially when traffic is heavy.</p>
    <p>You&rsquo;ll find the train station on B1, the basement level of the airport terminal. You can buy a ticket at the station before boarding, so there&rsquo;s no need to arrange transport in advance. It&rsquo;s still a good idea to have some Thai baht with you, just in case.</p>
    <p>The Airport Rail Link connects with other train lines in the city, making it easy to continue your journey to your hotel. Just keep in mind that Suvarnabhumi and Don Mueang are two different airports, so check which one you&rsquo;re arriving at before planning your transfer.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-getting-around" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <ArticleImage src={riverBoat} alt="A long-tail boat on the Chao Phraya River with Wat Ratchanatdaram in the background in Bangkok" caption="A boat on the Chao Phraya River" imgClass="aspect-[5/4] object-cover" />
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Getting Around Bangkok</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Bangkok has several ways to get around, including the BTS Skytrain, the MRT subway, buses, taxis, tuk-tuks and boats along the river and canals. With traffic often getting quite heavy, I&rsquo;d generally recommend using the trains whenever possible and taking a boat when it makes sense for your route.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">BTS Skytrain and MRT</h3>
    <p>The BTS Skytrain runs above the streets, while the MRT is the city&rsquo;s subway system. Both are useful for getting around Bangkok and avoiding traffic, but keep in mind that they operate separately, so tickets and payment methods aren&rsquo;t always the same.</p>
    <p>You can usually buy a single-journey ticket at the station before boarding. Depending on the system, you may receive a ticket card or a small plastic token. For the MRT, you tap the token at the entrance and return it through the slot at the exit gate. The BTS uses a different ticketing system, so follow the instructions at the station.</p>
    <p>One thing worth knowing is that Bangkok&rsquo;s public transport system works differently from what you may be used to in Europe. Don&rsquo;t assume that one ticket or travel card will work across every network, and keep some cash handy for ticket machines that don&rsquo;t accept your preferred payment method.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Boats Along the Chao Phraya River</h3>
    <p>Taking a boat is not only a lovely way to see Bangkok from the water, but it can also be a convenient way to get between certain attractions. For example, if you&rsquo;re visiting Wat Pho and want to cross the river to Wat Arun, you can take a short ferry ride between the two sides.</p>
    <p>Ticketing depends on the boat service. For some boats, you can buy your ticket at the pier, while on others you pay on board to the staff member collecting fares. Check the signs at the pier so you know which service you&rsquo;re taking.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Buses</h3>
    <p>Personally, I wouldn&rsquo;t make buses my first choice for getting around Bangkok. I tried using them on my first visit, and the bus I was waiting for never showed up. I gave them another try, but it wasn&rsquo;t an experience I&rsquo;d be keen to repeat.</p>
    <p>Some buses are newer and more comfortable, but many are older, and routes and arrival times can be less straightforward for visitors to navigate. Buses can be useful for certain journeys, but for a short trip to Bangkok, I&rsquo;d generally stick to the train network, boats, or a taxi when needed.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">Grab</h3>
    <p>I&rsquo;d recommend downloading the Grab app before your trip. It&rsquo;s really convenient for getting to places that aren&rsquo;t close to a train station, especially when you don&rsquo;t want to deal with Bangkok&rsquo;s traffic or figure out another way to get there. You can also use it to order food delivery straight to your hotel, which is handy after a long day of sightseeing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-best-things" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <ArticleImage src={goldenBuddha} alt="A woman looking at the giant golden Buddha statue at Wat Paknam Phasi Charoen in Bangkok" caption="The Big Buddha at Wat Paknam Phasi Charoen" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Best Things to Do in Bangkok</h2>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>1. Visit Bangkok&rsquo;s Temples</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If Bangkok doesn&rsquo;t win you over on its own, its hundreds of temples certainly will. Every time I visit the city, I add new temples to my list that I want to explore. And having already visited more than ten during my last three trips, I can confidently say that each temple has its own unique beauty, history, and atmosphere. With around 450 Buddhist temples throughout the city, you could easily call Bangkok the &ldquo;City of Temples.&rdquo; From the grand temples found in its most iconic locations to the smaller, lesser-known ones tucked away in its neighborhoods, temples are an integral part of Bangkok&rsquo;s identity.</p>
    <p><em>Useful tip: The word &ldquo;wat&rdquo; means temple.</em></p>
    <p><strong>Wat Pho</strong> &mdash; Wat Pho is home to the famous giant Reclining Buddha, a 46-meter-long statue covered in gold leaf. It&rsquo;s one of the temples I&rsquo;d recommend adding to your Bangkok itinerary, especially if you&rsquo;re visiting the city for the first time.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={watPhoReclining} alt="The golden Reclining Buddha statue at Wat Pho in Bangkok" caption="The Reclining Buddha at Wat Pho" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    <p><strong>Wat Arun</strong> &mdash; Set along the Chao Phraya River, Wat Arun is known for its tall, beautifully decorated central tower. I&rsquo;d recommend combining your visit with Wat Pho, as you can easily cross the river by ferry and visit both temples on the same day.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={watArunTemple} alt="The ornate spire of Wat Arun temple in Bangkok" caption="The central tower of Wat Arun" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    <p><strong>Wat Benchamabophit (The Marble Temple)</strong> &mdash; If you&rsquo;re looking for a quieter temple to visit, I&rsquo;d recommend Wat Benchamabophit. When I visited, I was actually the only person there, which made the experience even more special. It&rsquo;s also one of the most photogenic temples in Bangkok, with its white marble architecture and beautiful details that make it stand out from the city&rsquo;s other temples.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={watBenchamabophit} alt="A woman standing before the ornate marble entrance of Wat Benchamabophit in Bangkok" caption="Wat Benchamabophit, the Marble Temple" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>2. Try Local Thai Food</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Trying traditional Thai dishes is a must when visiting Bangkok and one of my top recommendations. Khao Soi is one of my personal favorites: a creamy curry noodle soup topped with crispy noodles, traditionally associated with Northern Thailand. Pad Thai is another well-known Thai dish, a stir-fried noodle dish that&rsquo;s popular throughout the country. And if you have a sweet tooth, don&rsquo;t miss Mango Sticky Rice, a delicious dessert made with ripe mango, sticky rice, and coconut milk.</p>
    <p><strong>Naam 1608</strong> &mdash; Naam 1608 is my favorite restaurant in Bangkok, and there&rsquo;s no way I&rsquo;d skip it on any of my visits to the city. Located right on the Chao Phraya River, it&rsquo;s a lovely spot to enjoy traditional Thai food while watching the boats pass by and the sun set over the water.</p>
    <p>I&rsquo;d strongly recommend making a reservation, especially if you want a table with a river view around sunset. During my last visit, the second floor was closed, apparently for maintenance, which was disappointing because it had my favorite view. If you can, ask about the seating options when booking so you know what to expect.</p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 md:gap-4">
      <ArticleImage src={naam1608Entrance} alt="The leafy entrance of Naam 1608 restaurant in Bangkok" caption="The entrance of Naam 1608" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={naam1608Sunset} alt="Thai food and drinks on a table overlooking the Chao Phraya River at sunset at Naam 1608" caption="Sunset views at Naam 1608" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>3. Discover Talat Noi</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleImage src={talatNoiMural} alt="Colorful street art mural along a street in Talat Noi, Bangkok" caption="Street art in Talat Noi" imgClass="aspect-[5/4] object-cover" />
    <p>Talat Noi is one of those neighborhoods I love visiting whenever I return to Bangkok. I enjoy wandering through its narrow streets, spotting colorful graffiti and street art, and finding cute little corners along the way.</p>
    <p>One of the most popular photo spots here is the old, rusty Fiat parked outside 32Bar X, a small shop specializing in Thai craft chocolate. You can stop by to try their chocolate drinks, although I have to admit that I wasn&rsquo;t particularly impressed by the chocolate myself. If you enjoy taking photos, though, it&rsquo;s worth stopping by to see the famous car that so many visitors come here to photograph.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={talatNoiFiat} alt="An old rusty Fiat parked outside 32Bar X in Talat Noi, Bangkok" caption="The famous rusty Fiat outside 32Bar X" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>4. Visit a Bangkok Park</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Visiting one of Bangkok&rsquo;s parks is a great way to take a break from the busy streets and see a different side of the city. Two parks I&rsquo;d recommend adding to your itinerary are Lumphini Park and Benjakitti Park, as each has something different to offer.</p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 md:gap-4">
      <ArticleImage src={lumphiniPavilion} alt="A traditional pavilion by the lake at Lumphini Park in Bangkok" caption="A pavilion by the lake at Lumphini Park" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={lumphiniLake} alt="The lakeside path at Lumphini Park in Bangkok at sunset" caption="The lakeside at Lumphini Park" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p><strong>Lumphini Park</strong> &mdash; Lumphini Park is probably my favorite of the two. It&rsquo;s known for its large monitor lizards, which you can often spot near the lake, but there&rsquo;s more to it than that. I visited in the late afternoon, and I still remember how the golden sunlight filtered through the trees, giving the park a lovely, almost romantic atmosphere. If you have time, I&rsquo;d recommend visiting later in the day to enjoy the light and take a walk around the lake.</p>
    <p>You might spot some of the park&rsquo;s famous monitor lizards, too. They can look intimidating because of their size, but they generally avoid people and aren&rsquo;t usually aggressive. They&rsquo;re wild animals, though, so keep a respectful distance, never try to touch or feed them, and give them space to move away.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={benjakittiPark} alt="The skyline of Bangkok reflected in the lake at Benjakitti Park" caption="Benjakitti Park, with the city skyline reflected in the lake" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    <p><strong>Benjakitti Park</strong> &mdash; If you&rsquo;re looking for a park with great views of Bangkok&rsquo;s skyline, I&rsquo;d recommend Benjakitti Park. It has a large lake where you can capture beautiful reflections of the surrounding skyscrapers, which makes it a lovely spot for photography. The newer forest park also has elevated walkways that take you above the wetlands, offering a different view of the city. If you&rsquo;re visiting in the late afternoon, the light reflecting off the water and the buildings can make for some really beautiful photos.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>5. Explore Bangkok&rsquo;s Shopping Malls</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>One thing I definitely wasn&rsquo;t prepared for on my first visit to Bangkok was just how many shopping malls the city has, and how spectacular they are. I honestly don&rsquo;t think I&rsquo;ve seen shopping malls quite like these anywhere else. They&rsquo;re huge, and many of them have their own unique character, so visiting them can be about much more than shopping.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={terminal21} alt="The exterior of Terminal 21 shopping mall in Bangkok" caption="Terminal 21 in Bangkok" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    <p><strong>Terminal 21</strong> &mdash; Terminal 21 is one of my personal favorites. Each floor is inspired by a different city around the world, so you can go from one destination to another as you explore the mall. And if you enjoy going to the movies, I&rsquo;d definitely recommend checking out the cinema here. I watched a movie there and had such a great experience that it ended up being one of my favorite parts of the visit.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={iconsiamWaterfall} alt="The indoor waterfall at ICONSIAM shopping mall in Bangkok" caption="The indoor waterfall at ICONSIAM" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    <p><strong>ICONSIAM</strong> &mdash; ICONSIAM is another mall I absolutely loved. It&rsquo;s one of the most impressive shopping malls I&rsquo;ve visited, with so much to see beyond the shops. One of its standout features is the indoor waterfall, and there&rsquo;s also an outdoor terrace where you can step outside and enjoy views of the Chao Phraya River. Even if shopping isn&rsquo;t really your thing, I&rsquo;d still recommend visiting just to see the place for yourself.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>6. Visit a Sky Bar</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleImage src={skyBarSkyline} alt="Bangkok's skyline at dusk seen from a rooftop bar" caption="Bangkok's skyline at dusk" imgClass="aspect-[5/4] object-cover" />
    <p>I&rsquo;m not really a night person, and since I don&rsquo;t drink alcohol, bars have never been places I&rsquo;m particularly drawn to. But Bangkok changed my mind when it came to sky bars. The city looks so beautiful from above, especially around sunset, and visiting a rooftop bar turned out to be a much more memorable experience than I expected.</p>
    <p>Some rooftop bars also host themed parties, complete with special dress codes, live music, and performances. I happened to visit Pastel Rooftop Bar during one of these events, with a blue-and-white theme, and there was even a show. It felt like a whole experience in itself, and honestly, it was on another level from what I&rsquo;d expected.</p>
    <ArticleImage src={pastelCocktail} alt="A mocktail on a table overlooking the Bangkok skyline at Pastel Rooftop Bar" caption="A mocktail with a view at Pastel Rooftop Bar" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    <p><strong>Pastel Rooftop Bar &amp; Mediterranean Dining</strong> &mdash; Located on the 22nd floor of Aira Hotel, Pastel combines Mediterranean-inspired dining with skyline views and a lively atmosphere. If you&rsquo;re thinking of visiting, I&rsquo;d recommend checking their social media beforehand to see whether there&rsquo;s a themed event during your stay. You might get to enjoy something a little different from the usual rooftop bar visit.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-7" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>7. Take a Day Trip from Bangkok</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Two of my top recommendations are Ancient City and the Sanctuary of Truth, as both offer something quite different from what you&rsquo;ll find in Bangkok itself.</p>
    <p><strong>Ancient City (Muang Boran)</strong> &mdash; Ancient City is a huge open-air museum featuring replicas and reconstructions of temples, palaces, and other important landmarks from different parts of Thailand. The grounds are enormous, so there&rsquo;s plenty to explore, and you can rent a golf cart to get around more easily.</p>
