<section id="section-8" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>8. Erawan Museum</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>If you&rsquo;re looking for something a little different to add to your Bangkok itinerary, I&rsquo;d recommend visiting the Erawan Museum. It&rsquo;s best known for the enormous three-headed elephant sculpture on top of the building, which makes it stand out from the city&rsquo;s more traditional temples. Inside, you&rsquo;ll find colorful stained glass, intricate decorations, and beautifully designed interiors that are just as interesting to explore as the exterior. It&rsquo;s also a great spot for photography if you enjoy visiting places with unique architecture.</p>
    <p>One of the most popular spots to photograph the museum is from the 7-Eleven across the street, where you can capture the impressive three-headed elephant sculpture above the building.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-9" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>9. Take a Night Cruise on the Chao Phraya River</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>One of the best ways to see Bangkok from a different perspective is to take a night cruise along the Chao Phraya River. As you sail past the city&rsquo;s riverside landmarks, you&rsquo;ll get to see places like Wat Arun illuminated after dark, creating a completely different atmosphere from visiting during the day. It&rsquo;s also a lovely way to enjoy Bangkok&rsquo;s skyline and take photos from the water.</p>
    <p>Many cruises include a buffet dinner and live entertainment, making it an easy option for an evening out. If you&rsquo;re planning to book one, I&rsquo;d recommend checking what&rsquo;s included and reading recent reviews, as the experience can vary depending on the boat you choose.</p>
