<section id="section-faq" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">FAQ</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={`${prose} space-y-6`}>
    <div id="faq-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">1. What is the best time to visit Bangkok?</h3>
      <p>The best time to visit Bangkok is generally from November to February, when the weather is cooler, less humid, and mostly dry, making it more comfortable to explore the city. March to May is much hotter, while the rainy season typically runs from May to October. Bangkok is a year-round destination, though, so you can still visit outside the peak season if you don&rsquo;t mind the heat or occasional rain.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">2. How many days do you need in Bangkok?</h3>
      <p>I&rsquo;d recommend spending at least 3 full days in Bangkok to explore its main temples, discover different neighborhoods, try local food, and experience the city beyond its most famous attractions. If you have 4&ndash;5 days, you&rsquo;ll have more time to explore at a relaxed pace or take a day trip outside the city.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">3. Is Bangkok easy to get around?</h3>
      <p>Bangkok can feel overwhelming at first, especially because of its heavy traffic, but getting around becomes much easier once you know your options. The BTS Skytrain and MRT subway are great for avoiding traffic, while river boats and the Grab app are useful for reaching places that aren&rsquo;t close to a train station. I&rsquo;d recommend using public transportation whenever possible and allowing extra time for journeys by road.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">4. Is Bangkok expensive to visit?</h3>
      <p>Bangkok can be surprisingly affordable, especially if you eat at local restaurants and street food stalls and use public transportation. At the same time, the city has plenty of upscale hotels, fine-dining restaurants, rooftop bars, and luxury shopping malls if you prefer a more luxurious trip. Your overall budget will depend largely on where you stay and how you choose to spend your time.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">5. Is Bangkok safe for tourists?</h3>
      <p>Bangkok is a popular destination for tourists, but, as in any major city, it&rsquo;s important to stay aware of your surroundings. Keep an eye on your belongings in crowded areas, use reputable transportation services, and be cautious of common tourist scams, especially offers that seem too good to be true. With sensible precautions, you can enjoy exploring the city while minimizing common travel risks.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-5" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">6. What should you wear when visiting temples in Bangkok?</h3>
      <p>When visiting Bangkok&rsquo;s temples, it&rsquo;s important to dress respectfully. Choose clothing that covers your shoulders and knees, and avoid very short shorts, crop tops, or revealing outfits. Some temples have stricter dress codes than others, so it&rsquo;s a good idea to carry a lightweight scarf or cover-up just in case. You&rsquo;ll also need to remove your shoes before entering many temple buildings.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">7. Do you need cash in Bangkok?</h3>
      <p>Yes, I&rsquo;d recommend carrying some Thai baht with you, even though cards are accepted at many hotels, shopping malls, and larger restaurants. Cash is still useful for street food stalls, local markets, smaller shops, and some transportation services. You can withdraw money from ATMs throughout the city, but remember that local ATM fees may apply to foreign cards.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-before-you-go" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Before you go">
      <p>Maybe Bangkok isn&rsquo;t the kind of city you fall in love with at first sight, but one thing is certain: you won&rsquo;t run out of things to be amazed by. From centuries-old temples and vibrant street food scenes to spectacular shopping malls, rooftop bars, and hidden little gems tucked away in its neighborhoods, Bangkok brings together so many different worlds in one city. And that&rsquo;s part of what makes it so fascinating: no matter what you enjoy, there&rsquo;s always something waiting to surprise you.</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>
  </div>
</section>
